  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3x)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3x)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3x+x)+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3x-x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4x)+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3x)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4x)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(0))-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(-1-1)-\\frac{1}{4}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
